        "question": {
          ":question/id": "6ce36812-54e7-588e-9015-339804c7db5c",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "The nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ start at $O$, with $|\\mathbf{u}|=7 \\sqrt{2}$ and $|\\mathbf{v}|=11 \\sqrt{2}$. The diagram shows their counterclockwise inclinations from the same reference ray $r$. The diagram is not to scale.\n\n![Vectors u and v at inclinations 165 degrees and 45 degrees from r, respectively.](assets/projection-4b626c400ba74efa7fba.png)\n\nFind the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{v}$ onto $\\mathbf{u}$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{u}}\\mathbf{v}=$\n{{field-1}}",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Both inclinations are measured counterclockwise from the same ray. Subtract the smaller inclination from the larger to find the included angle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta &=165^\\circ-45^\\circ=120^\\circ.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe project $\\mathbf{v}$, so we use $|\\mathbf{v}|=11 \\sqrt{2}$. The magnitude of $\\mathbf{u}$ does not enter the formula.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{u}}\\mathbf{v} &=|\\mathbf{v}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=11 \\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(120^\\circ\\right) \\\\\n&=11 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar projection is negative because the included angle is obtuse.",
